$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 09/Practice
::HC-W9-P01::A suspected measles outbreak is under investigation and laboratory confirmation is incomplete. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=communication under uncertainty#Correct. Uncertainty should be acknowledged without delaying useful protective guidance.
~absence of empathy#Not the best answer. Uncertainty should be acknowledged without delaying useful protective guidance.
~coordination and data-definition failure#Not the best answer. Uncertainty should be acknowledged without delaying useful protective guidance.
~late communication#Not the best answer. Uncertainty should be acknowledged without delaying useful protective guidance.
}

::HC-W9-P02::A suspected measles outbreak is under investigation and laboratory confirmation is incomplete. What is the strongest next response? {
=State what is known, what is being tested, what people should do now and when the next update will come.#Correct. Uncertainty should be acknowledged without delaying useful protective guidance.
~Acknowledge harm and concern before presenting essential technical information and actions.#Not the best answer. Uncertainty should be acknowledged without delaying useful protective guidance.
~Agree on definitions and reporting times or clearly explain why counts differ.#Not the best answer. Uncertainty should be acknowledged without delaying useful protective guidance.
~Communicate early with provisional facts, protective actions and explicit uncertainty.#Not the best answer. Uncertainty should be acknowledged without delaying useful protective guidance.
}

::HC-W9-P03::After flood deaths, an official opens a briefing with technical hydrology data and no acknowledgement of loss. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=absence of empathy#Correct. People process risk information in an emotional and social context.
~coordination and data-definition failure#Not the best answer. People process risk information in an emotional and social context.
~late communication#Not the best answer. People process risk information in an emotional and social context.
~false reassurance#Not the best answer. People process risk information in an emotional and social context.
}

::HC-W9-P04::After flood deaths, an official opens a briefing with technical hydrology data and no acknowledgement of loss. What is the strongest next response? {
=Acknowledge harm and concern before presenting essential technical information and actions.#Correct. People process risk information in an emotional and social context.
~Agree on definitions and reporting times or clearly explain why counts differ.#Not the best answer. People process risk information in an emotional and social context.
~Communicate early with provisional facts, protective actions and explicit uncertainty.#Not the best answer. People process risk information in an emotional and social context.
~Use calibrated language that reflects current evidence and describe what would change the assessment.#Not the best answer. People process risk information in an emotional and social context.
}

::HC-W9-P05::Two agencies release different case counts from different cut-off times without explanation. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=coordination and data-definition failure#Correct. Unexplained inconsistency damages credibility.
~late communication#Not the best answer. Unexplained inconsistency damages credibility.
~false reassurance#Not the best answer. Unexplained inconsistency damages credibility.
~failure to address audience information needs#Not the best answer. Unexplained inconsistency damages credibility.
}

::HC-W9-P06::Two agencies release different case counts from different cut-off times without explanation. What is the strongest next response? {
=Agree on definitions and reporting times or clearly explain why counts differ.#Correct. Unexplained inconsistency damages credibility.
~Communicate early with provisional facts, protective actions and explicit uncertainty.#Not the best answer. Unexplained inconsistency damages credibility.
~Use calibrated language that reflects current evidence and describe what would change the assessment.#Not the best answer. Unexplained inconsistency damages credibility.
~Answer the practical water-safety question with available evidence and clear actions.#Not the best answer. Unexplained inconsistency damages credibility.
}

::HC-W9-P07::Officials wait three days for complete evidence while rumours fill the information gap. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=late communication#Correct. Silence can create an information vacuum.
~false reassurance#Not the best answer. Silence can create an information vacuum.
~failure to address audience information needs#Not the best answer. Silence can create an information vacuum.
~hidden correction#Not the best answer. Silence can create an information vacuum.
}

::HC-W9-P08::Officials wait three days for complete evidence while rumours fill the information gap. What is the strongest next response? {
=Communicate early with provisional facts, protective actions and explicit uncertainty.#Correct. Silence can create an information vacuum.
~Use calibrated language that reflects current evidence and describe what would change the assessment.#Not the best answer. Silence can create an information vacuum.
~Answer the practical water-safety question with available evidence and clear actions.#Not the best answer. Silence can create an information vacuum.
~Announce the update, explain what new evidence changed the guidance and state the current action clearly.#Not the best answer. Silence can create an information vacuum.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 09/Mastery
::HC-W9-M01::A spokesperson says there is zero risk of further cases although contact tracing is incomplete. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=false reassurance#Correct. Overcertainty can destroy trust when evidence evolves.
~failure to address audience information needs#Not the best answer. Overcertainty can destroy trust when evidence evolves.
~hidden correction#Not the best answer. Overcertainty can destroy trust when evidence evolves.
~output-only crisis evaluation#Not the best answer. Overcertainty can destroy trust when evidence evolves.
}

::HC-W9-M02::A spokesperson says there is zero risk of further cases although contact tracing is incomplete. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use calibrated language that reflects current evidence and describe what would change the assessment.#Correct. Overcertainty can destroy trust when evidence evolves.
~Answer the practical water-safety question with available evidence and clear actions.#Not the best answer. Overcertainty can destroy trust when evidence evolves.
~Announce the update, explain what new evidence changed the guidance and state the current action clearly.#Not the best answer. Overcertainty can destroy trust when evidence evolves.
~Track reach, comprehension, trust, questions, protective action and operational consequences.#Not the best answer. Overcertainty can destroy trust when evidence evolves.
}

::HC-W9-M03::Residents repeatedly ask whether drinking water is safe, but briefings focus only on disease case counts. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=failure to address audience information needs#Correct. Relevant communication follows the decisions people are trying to make.
~hidden correction#Not the best answer. Relevant communication follows the decisions people are trying to make.
~output-only crisis evaluation#Not the best answer. Relevant communication follows the decisions people are trying to make.
~silence misread as reassurance#Not the best answer. Relevant communication follows the decisions people are trying to make.
}

::HC-W9-M04::Residents repeatedly ask whether drinking water is safe, but briefings focus only on disease case counts. What is the strongest next response? {
=Answer the practical water-safety question with available evidence and clear actions.#Correct. Relevant communication follows the decisions people are trying to make.
~Announce the update, explain what new evidence changed the guidance and state the current action clearly.#Not the best answer. Relevant communication follows the decisions people are trying to make.
~Track reach, comprehension, trust, questions, protective action and operational consequences.#Not the best answer. Relevant communication follows the decisions people are trying to make.
~Check response performance and use other feedback sources before concluding concern has fallen.#Not the best answer. Relevant communication follows the decisions people are trying to make.
}

::HC-W9-M05::A health ministry changes isolation guidance after new evidence but quietly edits the website without explaining the change. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=hidden correction#Correct. Transparent correction supports credibility.
~output-only crisis evaluation#Not the best answer. Transparent correction supports credibility.
~silence misread as reassurance#Not the best answer. Transparent correction supports credibility.
~fear without efficacy#Not the best answer. Transparent correction supports credibility.
}

::HC-W9-M06::A health ministry changes isolation guidance after new evidence but quietly edits the website without explaining the change. What is the strongest next response? {
=Announce the update, explain what new evidence changed the guidance and state the current action clearly.#Correct. Transparent correction supports credibility.
~Track reach, comprehension, trust, questions, protective action and operational consequences.#Not the best answer. Transparent correction supports credibility.
~Check response performance and use other feedback sources before concluding concern has fallen.#Not the best answer. Transparent correction supports credibility.
~Pair clear risk information with specific feasible protective actions and support.#Not the best answer. Transparent correction supports credibility.
}

::HC-W9-M07::A crisis team measures success only by number of press releases. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=output-only crisis evaluation#Correct. Volume of communication does not establish effectiveness.
~silence misread as reassurance#Not the best answer. Volume of communication does not establish effectiveness.
~fear without efficacy#Not the best answer. Volume of communication does not establish effectiveness.
~spokesperson boundary failure#Not the best answer. Volume of communication does not establish effectiveness.
}

::HC-W9-M08::A crisis team measures success only by number of press releases. What is the strongest next response? {
=Track reach, comprehension, trust, questions, protective action and operational consequences.#Correct. Volume of communication does not establish effectiveness.
~Check response performance and use other feedback sources before concluding concern has fallen.#Not the best answer. Volume of communication does not establish effectiveness.
~Pair clear risk information with specific feasible protective actions and support.#Not the best answer. Volume of communication does not establish effectiveness.
~State limits, defer to the appropriate expert and commit to follow-up rather than speculate.#Not the best answer. Volume of communication does not establish effectiveness.
}

::HC-W9-M09::A rumour hotline becomes quieter after officials stop answering calls promptly. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=silence misread as reassurance#Correct. Reduced questions can reflect disengagement or loss of trust.
~fear without efficacy#Not the best answer. Reduced questions can reflect disengagement or loss of trust.
~spokesperson boundary failure#Not the best answer. Reduced questions can reflect disengagement or loss of trust.
~unclear update rhythm#Not the best answer. Reduced questions can reflect disengagement or loss of trust.
}

::HC-W9-M10::A rumour hotline becomes quieter after officials stop answering calls promptly. What is the strongest next response? {
=Check response performance and use other feedback sources before concluding concern has fallen.#Correct. Reduced questions can reflect disengagement or loss of trust.
~Pair clear risk information with specific feasible protective actions and support.#Not the best answer. Reduced questions can reflect disengagement or loss of trust.
~State limits, defer to the appropriate expert and commit to follow-up rather than speculate.#Not the best answer. Reduced questions can reflect disengagement or loss of trust.
~Set predictable update times even when there is little new information.#Not the best answer. Reduced questions can reflect disengagement or loss of trust.
}

::HC-W9-M11::Officials use frightening images to force compliance with an evacuation message but provide no feasible route information. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=fear without efficacy#Correct. Threat information is more useful when people know what they can do.
~spokesperson boundary failure#Not the best answer. Threat information is more useful when people know what they can do.
~unclear update rhythm#Not the best answer. Threat information is more useful when people know what they can do.
~selection bias in trust evaluation#Not the best answer. Threat information is more useful when people know what they can do.
}

::HC-W9-M12::Officials use frightening images to force compliance with an evacuation message but provide no feasible route information. What is the strongest next response? {
=Pair clear risk information with specific feasible protective actions and support.#Correct. Threat information is more useful when people know what they can do.
~State limits, defer to the appropriate expert and commit to follow-up rather than speculate.#Not the best answer. Threat information is more useful when people know what they can do.
~Set predictable update times even when there is little new information.#Not the best answer. Threat information is more useful when people know what they can do.
~Use broader sampling and multiple channels before generalising trust estimates.#Not the best answer. Threat information is more useful when people know what they can do.
}

::HC-W9-M13::A technical expert answers every question even when topics fall outside their expertise. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=spokesperson boundary failure#Correct. Credibility includes knowing when not to guess.
~unclear update rhythm#Not the best answer. Credibility includes knowing when not to guess.
~selection bias in trust evaluation#Not the best answer. Credibility includes knowing when not to guess.
~post hoc causal inference#Not the best answer. Credibility includes knowing when not to guess.
}

::HC-W9-M14::A technical expert answers every question even when topics fall outside their expertise. What is the strongest next response? {
=State limits, defer to the appropriate expert and commit to follow-up rather than speculate.#Correct. Credibility includes knowing when not to guess.
~Set predictable update times even when there is little new information.#Not the best answer. Credibility includes knowing when not to guess.
~Use broader sampling and multiple channels before generalising trust estimates.#Not the best answer. Credibility includes knowing when not to guess.
~Consider natural epidemic dynamics and concurrent control measures before attributing the decline to communication.#Not the best answer. Credibility includes knowing when not to guess.
}

::HC-W9-M15::A district promises updates as soon as possible but then remains silent for 36 hours. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=unclear update rhythm#Correct. Regular communication reduces uncertainty and information voids.
~selection bias in trust evaluation#Not the best answer. Regular communication reduces uncertainty and information voids.
~post hoc causal inference#Not the best answer. Regular communication reduces uncertainty and information voids.
~inequitable action guidance#Not the best answer. Regular communication reduces uncertainty and information voids.
}

::HC-W9-M16::A district promises updates as soon as possible but then remains silent for 36 hours. What is the strongest next response? {
=Set predictable update times even when there is little new information.#Correct. Regular communication reduces uncertainty and information voids.
~Use broader sampling and multiple channels before generalising trust estimates.#Not the best answer. Regular communication reduces uncertainty and information voids.
~Consider natural epidemic dynamics and concurrent control measures before attributing the decline to communication.#Not the best answer. Regular communication reduces uncertainty and information voids.
~Provide realistic alternatives and support for people who cannot follow the ideal recommendation.#Not the best answer. Regular communication reduces uncertainty and information voids.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 09/Institutional
::HC-W9-I01::A survey after an emergency finds high trust among respondents reached through the ministry’s own social-media followers. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=selection bias in trust evaluation#Correct. Followers of an official account may differ systematically from non-followers.
~post hoc causal inference#Not the best answer. Followers of an official account may differ systematically from non-followers.
~inequitable action guidance#Not the best answer. Followers of an official account may differ systematically from non-followers.
~communication under uncertainty#Not the best answer. Followers of an official account may differ systematically from non-followers.
}

::HC-W9-I02::A survey after an emergency finds high trust among respondents reached through the ministry’s own social-media followers. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use broader sampling and multiple channels before generalising trust estimates.#Correct. Followers of an official account may differ systematically from non-followers.
~Consider natural epidemic dynamics and concurrent control measures before attributing the decline to communication.#Not the best answer. Followers of an official account may differ systematically from non-followers.
~Provide realistic alternatives and support for people who cannot follow the ideal recommendation.#Not the best answer. Followers of an official account may differ systematically from non-followers.
~State what is known, what is being tested, what people should do now and when the next update will come.#Not the best answer. Followers of an official account may differ systematically from non-followers.
}

::HC-W9-I03::A new warning is issued after cases rise, and officials conclude the warning caused subsequent decline. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=post hoc causal inference#Correct. Temporal sequence alone does not prove communication effect.
~inequitable action guidance#Not the best answer. Temporal sequence alone does not prove communication effect.
~communication under uncertainty#Not the best answer. Temporal sequence alone does not prove communication effect.
~absence of empathy#Not the best answer. Temporal sequence alone does not prove communication effect.
}

::HC-W9-I04::A new warning is issued after cases rise, and officials conclude the warning caused subsequent decline. What is the strongest next response? {
=Consider natural epidemic dynamics and concurrent control measures before attributing the decline to communication.#Correct. Temporal sequence alone does not prove communication effect.
~Provide realistic alternatives and support for people who cannot follow the ideal recommendation.#Not the best answer. Temporal sequence alone does not prove communication effect.
~State what is known, what is being tested, what people should do now and when the next update will come.#Not the best answer. Temporal sequence alone does not prove communication effect.
~Acknowledge harm and concern before presenting essential technical information and actions.#Not the best answer. Temporal sequence alone does not prove communication effect.
}

::HC-W9-I05::A message tells everyone to stay home during an outbreak without acknowledging people who must leave home for food or work. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=inequitable action guidance#Correct. Actionability depends on resources and social conditions.
~communication under uncertainty#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on resources and social conditions.
~absence of empathy#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on resources and social conditions.
~coordination and data-definition failure#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on resources and social conditions.
}

::HC-W9-I06::A message tells everyone to stay home during an outbreak without acknowledging people who must leave home for food or work. What is the strongest next response? {
=Provide realistic alternatives and support for people who cannot follow the ideal recommendation.#Correct. Actionability depends on resources and social conditions.
~State what is known, what is being tested, what people should do now and when the next update will come.#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on resources and social conditions.
~Acknowledge harm and concern before presenting essential technical information and actions.#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on resources and social conditions.
~Agree on definitions and reporting times or clearly explain why counts differ.#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on resources and social conditions.
}

